A||||5||Mark Matthews||Compression Question||||5||
Z||000000||Mark Matthews||12-05-2001||10:51 PM||||What the heck would cause a cylinder to have 150psi?!?!  I tried to do the compression test on the car but it got realy cold and dark.  I'm not familier with the firing order of the V6 Fiero's nor do I know which one is which number.  I only had time to check the compression on the center clinder at the back of the car.  It rang up to 150psi!  I'm gonna have to check the rest with that number, but that seems insane!<P>I also got ECM code 32, I'm not familier with the location of the EGR tubes either.<P>It looked like the vavle cover at the back of the car was leaking, dipstick oil was clean.<P>Everything else checked out awesome tho.  This car is pretty damn good.<P>Could carbon buildup cause a reading of 150?  Lets say they all read around 150, is this like complete engine tear down, or clean up here and there?<P>------------------<BR>Mark Matthews<BR>'70 BMW 2002<BR>'57 Chevy BelAir||||reg||5
Z||000001||zeelebuba||12-05-2001||11:04 PM||||150 should be ok.,If not a little low. What you want is even pressure across all cylinders,withen 10-15 psi ether way.<BR>  Cam timing can also affect cyl pressure.<BR>example- my 3300 engine in my fiero has 180psi which indicates a short cam/low rpm tq curve<BR>       My 71 gto 400 - 11.5 compression and a big cam is only around 150PSI||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mark Matthews||12-05-2001||11:25 PM||||That makes me feel a whole lot better, someone posted that 135 was high.  Thanks Z!<P>Could the EGR also cause the idle to wave up and down?  You know, the RPMs up then down, wup down.... etc....<P>------------------<BR>Mark Matthews<BR>'70 BMW 2002<BR>'57 Chevy BelAir||||reg||1
Z||000003||camon||12-06-2001||12:33 AM||||That 150 seems aweful high to me I'm only going by what I have read and by my own compression test (my car does have 110,000 mile on it though).<P>My compression is no lower then 112 and no higher then 115 between all cylinders. Your 150 might be ok...it just sounds high to me. <P>just my .02!<P>Edited in:<BR>Ohh yeah.......A bad EGR valve or EGR leak would cause flucuation in the rpm's. The EGR tube runs from the EGR valve (the metal disc like thing near the distribitor cap) to the underside of the plenum (the big thing that says fiero on top of your injectors)! The EGR tube should be wrapped in some type of insulation insulation and is hard to miss!<BR>------------------<BR>   Keith<BR>1987 Fiero GT<P>[This message has been edited by camon (edited 12-06-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by camon (edited 12-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||tgowens||12-06-2001||10:11 AM||||150 is ok, it just means that the rings on that piston are good and you have good compression in that cylinder (by the way, that's the #4 cylinder - the #1 cylinder is on the passenger side of the car and closest to the trunk, the #2 is on the passenger side closest to the rear window, #3 middle/trunk, #4 middle/window, #5 driver's/trunk, #6 driver's/window.........)<P>The EGR system consists of the EGR valve, the EGR tube, vacuum lines running under the plenum, and the EGR selinoid itself. If you have a vacuum leak (such as a cracked EGR tube) you will generally experience high RPM's. If you are showing a code 32 but the tach reads normal (around 1000 to 1100 RPM's) I would suspect the EGR selenoid - they do fail frequently. Normally the Fiero sill surge 100 RPM's or so at idle but if you are experiencing any more than that I would suspect the Idle Air Control motor (IAC) - this is located on the throttle body and is the lower of the two plugs from the wiring harness, the upper plug is the Throttle Position Sensor (TPS)<P>Hope this helps........<P>Don't worry about the 150, do worry when you have 0 compression!! Ideally, compression will be within 10 to 15% of the adjacent cylinder .... <p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 12-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mark Matthews||12-06-2001||11:06 AM||||Thanks guys, this board is great!  I'm picking it up tonight!<P>------------------<BR>Mark Matthews<BR>'70 BMW 2002<BR>'57 Chevy BelAir||||reg||14
